(7780427, 1294193, 91496, '<td valign="top">
<table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Azrifel:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by glad2badad:</b></div><hr/><i> Is this a for real street?  Feels like a historical site.  Very colorful for sure.  A bit soft all around. </i></td></tr></table>
<br/>It is a historical street rebuild in an open air museum in Enkhuizen. It is soft because I cannot get good sharpening to work on small images. It is sharp as a knife out of the camera and auto-resized on facebook. Just not when I edit them myself. </i></td></tr></table>
<br/>
<br/>It is a little hard to tell but I think the focus point is on the buildings behind the lady (as the roof tiles look sharp).  Couple that with an aperture of f5.6 and your subject and all the foreground elements would be out of focus. f16 or f22 would have helped keep the whole scene sharp - bumping your iso to keep a shutter speed assuming you were handholding.</td>', 'Originally posted by Azrifel: Originally posted by glad2badad: Is this a for real street?  Feels like a historical site.  Very colorful for sure.  A bit soft all around. 
It is a historical street rebuild in an open air museum in Enkhuizen. It is soft because I cannot get good sharpening to work on small images. It is sharp as a knife out of the camera and auto-resized on facebook. Just not when I edit them myself. 

It is a little hard to tell but I think the focus point is on the buildings behind the lady (as the roof tiles look sharp).  Couple that with an aperture of f5.6 and your subject and all the foreground elements would be out of focus. f16 or f22 would have helped keep the whole scene sharp - bumping your iso to keep a shutter speed assuming you were handholding.', '2025-05-05 09:30:34.000000', NULL, 0),
